# CP13.1 — the class breakdown, the reconciliation finding, and a 10k run.
#
# F16. Every headline number is now the 10,000-case run SPEC §3 specifies. The
#      2,000-case figures are kept in the artefact's `comparison` block so the
#      divergence between scales is visible rather than something a reader has
#      to notice from a commit diff.
#
# F17. The class breakdown moved into README RESULTS. It is a fact about the
#      result, not a caveat on it.
#
# F18. The auditor is reframed around understatement. Reported-minus-reconciled
#      is negative for every arm: the dominant error is settlements the agent
#      never learned about, not recovery it claimed and did not get.
#
# F19. out/charts/metrics.json -> out/metrics.json, with its own negation in
#      .gitignore. A data file was living in an images directory because that
#      directory held the only negation available at CP13.
#
# F20. SPEC §19 extended to eight sections rather than deleting the Razorpay
#      material to satisfy a count fixed before CP12 existed.
#
# F21. The retry-timing figures are computed into the artefact by
#      `settle.eval.report.timing_block` instead of being quoted from train.py's
#      stdout. See the finding below — this is the fix that earned its keep.

README.md
KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md
settle/eval/charts.py
settle/eval/report.py
out/charts/
out/metrics.json
.gitignore
SPEC.md
PLAN.md
tests/test_charts.py
checkpoints/cp13_1.allowlist

# --- F21 found that the figures it was auditing were wrong ------------------
#
# The README carried "median spread 3.7 points across eight offsets; timing
# features rank 26-37 of 45". Recomputing them with train.py's own parameters
# reproduces neither number, and the feature count is 46, not 45 — SPEC's A93
# says 46, and A93 is what changed them.
#
# The stale figures predate A93, which recomputed `days_since_last_attempt` at
# the dispatch moment rather than the decision tick and added
# `hours_to_contact_window`. They were carried forward from an older training
# log through CP13's prompt and into the README without anyone re-running the
# training that produced them.
#
# `train.py` also groups four features as "timing", and they are not one thing.
# A83's withdrawn claim was about reaching a customer's LIQUIDITY WINDOW —
# `day_of_month_at_dispatch`, `days_to_month_start`, `in_liquidity_window`.
# `days_since_last_attempt` measures recency, which is a different hypothesis
# and a far better feature. Reporting the four together understated one and
# overstated the other, so `timing_block` separates them and the README quotes
# the liquidity three.
#
# A83's conclusion survives: the liquidity features rank where the withdrawal
# said they would, and there is still no liquidity curve. What changed is that
# the numbers supporting it are now recomputed from a committed artefact and
# checked by a test, rather than quoted from a log nobody re-ran.
#
# This is the whole argument for CHT-3 in one incident. The figures were in the
# README for exactly one checkpoint before they were caught, and they were only
# caught because F21 forced them into an artefact.

# --- One note on `report.py` ------------------------------------------------
#
# Each arm now runs exactly once. `by_decline_class` took the runs rather than
# making its own, which at 10,000 cases removes three redundant arm runs from
# the critical path. They must agree with the headline table by construction
# anyway; if they ever disagreed, the bug would have been in the duplication.
